# Smoke test for the pty builtin's `wait_quiet`/`run` primitives (see
# src/builtins/pty/session.rs's `PtySession::wait_quiet`).
#
# `run` is sugar for "submit this line, then wait until the screen stops changing" --
# it proves a command actually ran and the session is usable again afterward, without
# needing a specific string to match (unlike `wait_for`).
#
# A bare `wait_quiet` (no pattern) is also useful standalone: it replaces the blind
# `sleep 300ms` that every existing tests/pty_*.crush file
# uses after writing a command, with "wait until nothing new has arrived for a while"
# instead of "wait a fixed guess and hope it was long enough".
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)

$t:run "echo hello"
$t:wait_for "hello"

# The Ctrl-Z below is unrelated to what this file actually smoke-tests (`run`/
# `wait_quiet` above already covered that) -- it's just exercising that the session
# remains usable afterward. `pty_enable_counted_prompt`/`pty_send_control` (see
# pty_test_helpers.crush) are used for it rather than a bare `wait_for "# "`, for the
# same reason every other pty job-control test uses them now: a bare `wait_for "# "`
# can't actually prove the Ctrl-Z landed (a prompt already on screen satisfies it
# instantly), and `wait_quiet` alone can't either -- confirmed directly elsewhere in
# this investigation that `wait_quiet` reports "settled" just as readily when a
# control byte was silently lost and the job is simply still running quietly as when
# it actually worked.
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)
$t:write "sleep 100s\n"
$n = $(pty_send_control $t $n $t:control:z)
pty_assert_job_status $t 0 "Paused"

$t:close
echo "ok"
